<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Ozolian Locris</h1><p class="st-meta">700 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.) · capital Amphissa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Confederation of the western Locrians on the Gulf of Corinth, centred on Amphissa, whose clash with the Amphictyony brought Philip II into central Greece.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The western or Ozolian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/locrians">Locrians</a> lived on the northern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-corinth">Gulf of Corinth</a>, west of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/delphi">Delphi</a>, in small hill towns such as Amphissa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaleion">Chaleion</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oeantheia">Oeanthea</a>. They were related to the Locrians of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/opuntian-locris">Opuntian Locris</a> but separated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocis">Phocis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doris-greece">Doris</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafpaktos">Naupactus</a> was theirs until <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> gave it to Messenian refugees c. 456 BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> described the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/locrians">Locrians</a> as still carrying arms in daily life, a sign of archaic customs. Their towns were loosely confederated, with Amphissa the most important. A bronze tablet from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaleion">Chaleion</a> of the 5th century BCE regulates disputes between towns, a rare early example of Greek inter-state law.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When Amphissa farmed the sacred plain of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/delphi">Delphi</a>, the Amphictyony declared the Fourth Sacred War; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II</a> destroyed Amphissa in 338 BCE. Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/locris">Locris</a> later joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aetolian-league">Aetolian League</a> and came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 146 BCE.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="tribal confederation, 700 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">tribal confederation</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:45.77%;width:11.02%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Amphissa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Locrian Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Western Locris, Amphissa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-423&amp;focus=ozolian-locris" aria-label="Ozolian Locris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5236 795 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ozolian Locris lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5556 1051L5538 1049"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5538 1049h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5556 1051h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5556 1051h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Amphissa. <a href="/atlas?year=-423&amp;focus=ozolian-locris">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

